WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:07.120
What if there were a six-bedroom, two-bathroom&nbsp;
house with a gym and a gorgeous view that cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:14.080
$150 billion? You probably won't find that on&nbsp;
earth but it does exist a little farther away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.000
The International Space Station has more&nbsp;
livable room than a six-bedroom house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.960
It also has a couple of toilets, a&nbsp;
workout area, and a 360-degree view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:33.200
Humanity's only permanent outpost in space is the&nbsp;
most expensive object ever built. NASA has called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.120
it home for the last two decades but the managers&nbsp;
are planning on moving out - marking a new era&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:48.640
in space. Tonight, I am directing NASA to&nbsp;
develop a permanently manned space station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.840
and to do it within a decade. In 1984, President&nbsp;
Ronald Reagan approved the construction of freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.320
but because of budget constraints, it was never&nbsp;
built, and eventually, the vision morphed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.480
the ISS - the combined effort of five different&nbsp;
agencies representing 15 different nations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.880
In November 1998, the first component of&nbsp;
the space station launched from Baikonur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.960
Kazakhstan on top of a Proton rocket - the&nbsp;
Russian-built Zarya module wasn't alone for long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.360
Endeavor already traveling 575 mph. Two weeks&nbsp;
later, the shuttle Endeavor carried the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.520
module, Unity, the first American element built&nbsp;
by Boeing. When Zarya and Unity joined together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.440
that was the beginning of an engineering marvel.&nbsp;
Dozens of launches over many years followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.520
building the station piece by piece by piece,&nbsp;
fitting together tens of thousands of parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.760
The end result was a 109-meter-long structure the&nbsp;
length of an American football field. Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:56.160
it often seems the ISS is gliding at a slow pace,&nbsp;
it's actually moving at a speed of 17,500 mph - so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.240
fast that it can circle the planet every 90&nbsp;
minutes. As of the making of this video, 240&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.280
people have visited the large spacecraft, living&nbsp;
and working on what's really a giant floating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.400
laboratory, conducting research and science&nbsp;
experiments that have led to many inventions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.040
NASA's scratch resistant helmets created&nbsp;
scratch resistant lenses. The rubber molding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.320
in those helmets is found in the soles of athletic&nbsp;
shoes. The polymer fabric in spacesuits protects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:28.080
firefighters. Technology used to track astronauts'&nbsp;
eyes is essential for laser eye surgery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.840
NASA helped develop the first computer mouse and&nbsp;
the list goes on. But the main reason the ISS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:40.240
exists is to help humans learn about living and&nbsp;
working in space in order to send us even further.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.400
In 2018, President Trump signed a directive&nbsp;
to focus on returning astronauts to the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.720
It's been a while since we went&nbsp;
there. The last time was in 1972.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.280
NASA created the Artemis program to send the first&nbsp;
woman and next man to the lunar surface by 2024&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.720
using its Space Launch System which will be the&nbsp;
foundation for the agency's exploration beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.440
Earth's orbit. It's no coincidence Washington&nbsp;
initially proposed ending NASA's funding for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.480
the space station the same year it wants to go to&nbsp;
the moon - although funding can now be extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.000
for a few more years. NASA spends between $3&nbsp;
to $4 billion to maintain and operate the ISS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.280
every year - money that could be used to support&nbsp;
other projects. Its annual budget is about 1$9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.760
billion, although the Trump administration has&nbsp;
been increasing funding the last few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.960
providing nearly $23 billion in 2020. Even&nbsp;
before President Trump came into office, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.320
Obama administration tried to turn more of NASA's&nbsp;
responsibilities over to the private sector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:51.200
And liftoff. The final liftoff of Atlantis. When&nbsp;
the agency retired its Shuttle program in 2011.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.560
it decided not to build its own infrastructure&nbsp;
to get to the ISS but rather have its astronauts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.320
hitch a ride with commercial companies. NASA gave&nbsp;
billion-dollar contracts to Boeing and SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.320
through its Commercial CrewPprogram so they could&nbsp;
build their own spacecraft. Go NASA, go SpaceX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.760
godspeed Bob and Doug. Elon Musk's company&nbsp;
won the race against Boeing when it sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:20.080
two astronauts to the ISS aboard Crew Dragon in&nbsp;
May 2020 which I talked about in another video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.440
Boeing should have flown astronauts by now as&nbsp;
well but an uncrewed test flight in December 2019&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.080
didn't go as expected when the Starliner&nbsp;
spacecraft went into the wrong orbit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:36.640
so a crewed mission will have to wait until 2021.&nbsp;
Opening up the ISS to private companies is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:43.280
changing the way it looks. NASA gave the Texas&nbsp;
startup Axiom a $140 million-dollar contract to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.040
build a segment that can be attached to the space&nbsp;
station in 2024 and used for commercial business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.560
activities. When the ISS eventually retires,&nbsp;
that component will detach and operate freely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.720
as a private space station. This is what&nbsp;
one of the main living areas looks like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.440
Axiom is also teaming up with SpaceX to fly three&nbsp;
people to the space station in 2021. The price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.800
per seat is $55 million and one is already taken.&nbsp;
Hollywood is showing interest as well. Tom Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:18.720
could board Crew Dragon to film a movie at the&nbsp;
ISS. And Russia state corporation Roscosmos is set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.320
to get back into the space tourism industry with&nbsp;
flights to the ISS planned in the coming years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.960
But private trips are out of reach for most&nbsp;
people nor is this the main objective of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.000
these companies. It's a way of testing out&nbsp;
technology in the pursuit of greater goals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.160
SpaceX plans to circle the moon on a trip financed&nbsp;
by Japanese billionaire Yusaku Maezawa, who will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:48.400
also take a group of artists with them in 2023.&nbsp;
They'll be traveling on Starship, a fully reusable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.360
system currently in development. Starship will&nbsp;
eventually be modified so that it can land on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.360
moon. SpaceX is one of three companies selected&nbsp;
by NASA to design a lunar landing system competing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.680
against Jeff Bezos' Blue Origin and the relative&nbsp;
newcomer Dynetics. This is the agency's first step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:12.160
toward creating a long-term presence on the moon&nbsp;
with the aim of leading to an even greater goal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.640
Mars. NASA hopes to send humans to the red planet&nbsp;
in the 2030s but everything it wants to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:25.840
do here it must first do here. As the agency&nbsp;
keeps its eyes on the moon and off the ISS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.120
its partners appear to be doing the same. The&nbsp;
European Space Agency is working ever more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:38.240
closely with China. In 2022, China aims to build&nbsp;
its own space station and fly European astronauts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:43.680
U.S. Congress banned Beijing from the ISS due&nbsp;
to security concerns. And America's partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.800
Japan is teaming up with India on a mission to&nbsp;
the moon in order to counter the rise of China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.600
While everyone's attention is turned elsewhere,&nbsp;
the ISS is not getting any younger. Wear and tear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.320
on the 20-year-old structure means the end of&nbsp;
its life will be some time in the next decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.520
Going to the graveyard involves a controlled&nbsp;
destructive reentry into Earth's atmosphere just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:12.000
like this animation of the Russian space station&nbsp;
Mir deorbiting in 2001. The ISS will burn up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.040
break up, and vaporize into fragments, although&nbsp;
some parts would survive and fall into a stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.920
of empty ocean in the South Pacific. The total&nbsp;
weight of the debris is expected to be between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:32.080
53,500 to 173,000 pounds or as many as 32 Model&nbsp;
X's. Even when the ISS is no longer with us, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:38.640
will be remembered not only as the most expensive&nbsp;
man-made object ever built, not only as a triumph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:44.960
of international cooperation but as a building&nbsp;
block toward deeper missions in space. Without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:52.400
International Space Station, a new era of space&nbsp;
travel would not be possible Hey guys, I'm Cindy&nbsp;&nbsp;

